          <a href="#blower" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. When a Blower Wins</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">10. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">11. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          For most people the EGO 56V 12-inch shovel is the cordless snow shovel to buy. But the best one for you is usually the bare tool that runs on batteries you already own, and every pick here stops at about 6 inches of snow per pass.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="how-we-picked" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How We Picked These Cordless Snow Shovels
          </h2>

          <p>
            A cordless snow shovel is a narrow, single-stage auger on a long shaft. We already explain what that means, and what it can and cannot clear, in our guide to the <a href="/electric-snow-shovel/">electric snow shovel</a>. This page assumes you have decided you want one that runs on a battery and is about which model to buy. The short answer for most people is the EGO 56V 12-inch shovel. The longer answer depends almost entirely on which battery platform is already in your garage.
          </p>

          <p>
            We built the shortlist from shovels that are currently sold by the major battery-tool brands and that have published specifications we could check against the manufacturer or a retailer repeating them. We did not lab-test these shovels. Every width, depth, throw distance, weight and run-time figure below is attributed to the manufacturer or a named retailer, and where sources disagree we say so rather than picking the more flattering number.
          </p>

          <p>
            We weighted four things. Clearing depth matters most, because a power shovel cannot take more snow per pass than its intake is tall, and most in this category stop at about 6 inches. Weight matters next, since you carry these up steps and hold them in front of you. Then battery platform, because a bare tool on batteries you already own costs a fraction of a kit. Finally, rated run time, read with the cold-weather caveats in section 5.
          </p>

          <p>
            We left out corded shovels, which belong in the broader guide, and full snow blowers, which are a different tool. If your main job is a two-car driveway, start with our <a href="/best-electric-snow-blower/">best electric snow blowers</a> instead. And if you are still weighing a power shovel against a well-chosen manual one, our <a href="/best-snow-shovel/">best snow shovels</a> guide covers both, along with the pushers and scoops that still beat any motor on heavy, wet snow.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="platform" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Buy Into the Battery Platform You Already Own
          </h2>

          <p>
            The most important decision is not the shovel. It is the battery. Every pick here is sold either as a bare tool or as a kit with a battery and charger, and the battery is usually a large share of the kit price. If you already own a mower, leaf blower or string trimmer from EGO, Toro, Greenworks, Ryobi or DeWalt, the bare shovel on that platform will nearly always be the best value, even if another brand's shovel has a slightly better spec sheet.
          </p>

          <p>
            That is not a minor saving. A second battery also gives you something the specs cannot: you can swap a warm pack in when the first one sags in the cold, which roughly doubles the job you can do in one session. Owners of a battery mower already have a large battery sitting idle all winter. Using it in a snow shovel is exactly the kind of cross-season use these platforms are designed for.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you own no battery tools at all, the calculation changes. Then you are choosing a platform as much as a shovel, and it is worth thinking about the next three tools you might buy. EGO and Ryobi have very broad outdoor lineups. Toro is strong in mowers and snow blowers. Greenworks covers a lot at several voltages. Snow Joe's 24V system is cheaper but mostly limited to Snow Joe and Sun Joe tools, as our <a href="/snow-joe-snow-blower/">Snow Joe guide</a> explains.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do not choose on voltage alone. The voltage on the box is a platform label, not a performance score. An 80V shovel with a 12-inch intake still takes a 12-inch bite, and the depth limit is set by the housing, not the motor. Within this category, width, depth, weight and battery capacity tell you far more than whether it says 20V, 56V or 80V.
          </p>

