        <div id="runtime" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Run Time, Amp-Hours and the Cold
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every run-time figure on a cordless shovel listing is tied to a specific battery and, implicitly, to light, dry snow. Toro quotes up to 45 minutes on its 2.5Ah kit battery. DeWalt cites about 20 minutes on a 4Ah pack. Greenworks' 2.0Ah figure is listed as 25 minutes on one page and 45 on another. Those numbers are not comparable across brands, because each is measured differently and on different batteries.
          </p>

          <p>
            A more useful rough comparison is battery energy. Multiply the platform voltage by the amp-hours and you get an approximate watt-hour figure: a 56V 2.5Ah pack is about 140 watt-hours, a 24V 5.0Ah pack about 120. Treat that as a ballpark, not a spec, because brands quote maximum voltage on some platforms and nominal voltage on others. But it does explain why a big 24V pack can keep pace with a small high-voltage one, and why the battery you pair with a bare tool matters as much as the tool.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then the cold takes its share. Lithium-ion batteries deliver noticeably less of their capacity when they are cold, and wet snow is a heavy, constant load. Real run time in wet snow well below freezing can be a fraction of the number on the box. The practical fixes are simple: keep the battery indoors at room temperature until you head out, and bring a second battery if you have one. Most chargers will not charge a cold pack anyway, so let it warm up first.
          </p>

          <p>
            For sizing the job, think in passes rather than minutes. A 12-inch shovel clears a 4-foot walk in about four passes. If you have a long front walk, steps and a deck, the area adds up quickly, and that is where a 4Ah or 5Ah pack, or a second battery, earns its place. If you only clear a stoop and a short path, the smallest kit battery is plenty.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="seniors" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Weight, Handles and Choosing for Older Users
          </h2>

          <p>
            A common reason to buy a cordless snow shovel is to stop lifting snow, and for many older homeowners that is the whole point. Lifting heavy, wet snow is genuinely hard work. The American Heart Association has long cautioned that snow shovelling can put a significant strain on the heart, especially for people who are not used to exertion. A power shovel removes the lifting, but it is still physical work, so anyone with heart or back concerns should take the same advice: work in short sessions, dress for the cold, and check with a doctor about what is safe for them.
          </p>

          <p>
            For an older user, weight is the first spec to check. The listed weights here run from about 13 pounds for the Snow Joe kit and the bare DeWalt to around 18 pounds for the Greenworks and the assembled Ryobi wheeled model. That difference matters more than it sounds when you are holding the tool out in front of you on icy steps. Remember to add the battery: EGO, for example, lists 14 pounds bare and 16.7 pounds with its recommended battery.
          </p>

          <p>
            The second consideration is push versus carry. Most power shovels have no wheels, so you either hold their weight or let the housing slide along the ground. Ryobi's wheeled 12-inch model is the exception in this group, and on a long walkway that is a real advantage: you push it like a small mower rather than holding it up. On steps, wheels do not help, and a lighter carried tool is better.
          </p>

          <p>
            Look at the handles. A second, adjustable front handle lets you set a grip that keeps your back upright, and a folding or hinged shaft like the EGO's makes the tool easier to lift on and off a wall hook. Finally, think about where the battery sits and how easy it is to remove with gloves on. A tool that is awkward to load in a warm kitchen will be harder at the door in a storm.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE SHORT VERSION</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">For older users: lightest kit, or wheels for long walks.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">The Snow Joe 24V-SS13-XR is the lightest complete kit here at a listed 13.5 to 14 pounds. For a long front walk, the Ryobi 40V wheeled shovel lets you push instead of carry. For steps, pick the lightest tool you can find on a battery you own.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="width-depth" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Width, Depth and What They Really Mean
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every pick here clears 10 to 13 inches wide and about 6 inches deep per pass, with one listing for a Greenworks LED variant at 8 inches. Those numbers are close to the physical limits of the category, and they set expectations better than any adjective. Six inches is roughly the height of the intake. Snow deeper than that piles up in front of the housing rather than going through it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Width decides how many passes a job takes. A 12-inch shovel covers a 4-foot path in four passes; the 10-inch DeWalt needs five. On a single set of steps the difference is trivial. On a 60-foot walk it is the difference between a quick job and a noticeably longer one, and it multiplies the battery you need. The Snow Joe's 13-inch width is a small but real advantage on longer runs.
          </p>

          <p>
            Throw distance, listed between 20 and 30 feet here, is a best case for dry, fluffy snow. Wet snow lands closer, and wind can blow powder back at you. What matters more in practice is whether you can aim it. EGO's 60-degree dial and the rotating chute on Ryobi's RY40SN01K kit let you throw snow off the side of a staircase or away from a car, which a fixed chute cannot.
          </p>

          <p>
            If your storms regularly drop more than 6 inches at a time, no shovel here will keep up unless you go out during the storm and clear in layers. That is the honest limit of the category, and it is the point at which a full blower is the better buy, covered in section 8. Clearing early and often is the single habit that makes any power shovel work.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="steps-decks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Steps, Decks and Walkways
          </h2>

          <p>
            Steps are where a cordless shovel earns its price. A blower cannot climb stairs, and clearing them by hand means bending and lifting on the worst footing in the yard. With a power shovel you carry the tool up, run it across each tread and throw the snow off the side. Start at the top and work down, so loose snow falls onto treads you have not done yet rather than ones you have.
          </p>

          <p>
            Decks are the second strong case. Wood and composite boards do not take kindly to the steel scraper bars on heavier machines, and many owners will not drive a blower onto them at all. The rubber-edged augers on these shovels are much gentler. Keep an eye on raised screw heads and board ends, and check your decking manufacturer's guidance on snow removal tools if you are unsure what it allows.
          </p>

          <p>
            Walkways and side paths are the classic job, and here the platform and weight choices from earlier come together. A light tool with a big battery, or a wheeled one, is the right answer for a long front walk. A short path from the driveway to the door is easy for any of them. If you are also clearing a driveway with a blower, a power shovel is the natural partner for everything the blower cannot reach.
          </p>

          <p>
            Avoid gravel. Because the auger touches the surface to clean it, it will pick up and throw stones just as readily as snow, which is dangerous to windows, cars and people and hard on the auger. The same goes for any loose surface. On gravel, leave a thin layer of snow on the ground or use a tool with adjustable skid shoes.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="battery-care" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Battery Care Through the Winter
          </h2>

          <p>
            Battery care is the cheapest performance upgrade there is. Store batteries indoors, at room temperature, between storms. A battery left in a cold garage starts the job with less usable capacity and may refuse to charge until it warms up. Bring it out only when you are ready to start, and bring it back inside as soon as you finish.
          </p>

          <p>
            Charge after use, but let the pack warm up first. Most lithium-ion chargers on these platforms will not charge a pack that is too cold, and some will simply flash an error until it reaches a safe temperature. If you plan to clear several times during a long storm, set up a rotation: one battery in use, one charging indoors. That is often the real difference between a power shovel that keeps up and one that does not.
          </p>

          <p>
            At the end of winter, store batteries partly charged rather than full or empty, in a cool, dry place indoors, following the manufacturer's guidance. Many of these batteries will spend the summer in a mower or trimmer anyway, which is the best way to keep them in use. Check the battery terminals and the tool's battery port for corrosion or melted snow before each season.
          </p>

          <p>
            Look after the tool as well. Knock the snow off the housing after each use, let it dry before you hang it up, and check the auger paddles and chute for wear and cracks before the first storm. Our guide to <a href="/how-to-prepare-a-snow-blower-for-winter/">preparing a snow blower for winter</a> covers the same checks for full machines, and most of it applies here.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="blower" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. When a Snow Blower Beats a Cordless Shovel
          </h2>

          <p>
            A cordless shovel is the wrong main tool for a two-car or longer driveway. At 12 inches a pass, a 20-foot-wide driveway takes 20 passes, and on a long one that is more work than the shovel and its battery are designed for. A single-stage battery blower clears about twice the width per pass, and a two-stage machine handles depth and the plow berm that stop any shovel. Our <a href="/best-electric-snow-blower/">best electric snow blowers</a> list is the place to start.
          </p>

          <p>
            The plow berm at the end of the driveway is the clearest case. It is compacted, often partly frozen and usually well over a foot deep. It defeats single-stage blowers, let alone a power shovel. If clearing that ridge is part of every storm, you need a two-stage machine or a manual shovel and some time. Our <a href="/snow-removal-tools/">snow removal tools</a> guide covers the full kit, from pushers to ice choppers.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you already own a battery platform, look at its blower before the shovel. EGO's lineup, covered in our <a href="/ego-snow-blower/">EGO snow blower guide</a>, and Toro's, in our <a href="/toro-electric-snow-blower/">Toro guide</a>, both run on the same batteries as the shovels here. Greenworks and Ryobi also make battery blowers, which we cover in our <a href="/greenworks-snow-blower/">Greenworks</a> and <a href="/ryobi-snow-blower/">Ryobi snow blower</a> guides.
          </p>

          <p>
            For many households the right answer is both: a blower for the driveway and a cordless shovel for the steps, deck and walks. Sharing one battery platform between them is what makes that combination affordable. If your area is small and your storms are light, a cordless shovel alone may be all you need. Our <a href="/best-snow-shovel/">best snow shovels</a> guide covers the manual shovels worth keeping alongside it for the heavy, wet days.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a Kit When You Own the Batteries</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                If you already have a mower or blower on the same platform, the bare tool is usually far cheaper than the kit, and the battery you own is often bigger than the one in the box. Check the model suffix: Toro's 39909 includes a battery and the 39909T does not, and similar splits exist across brands.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting the Box Run Time in Real Snow</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Run-time figures assume light, dry snow and a warm battery. In wet snow well below freezing, real run time can be a fraction of the listed number. Store batteries indoors, carry a spare if you have one, and plan the job in passes rather than minutes on the clock.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Waiting Until the Storm Is Over</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Every pick here takes about 6 inches per pass. Wait for 10 inches to fall and you are clearing in two layers with a 12-inch tool. Going out during the storm, while the snow is still shallow and dry, is the single habit that makes a power shovel keep up.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running It on Gravel</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Because the auger touches the surface, it throws stones as readily as snow, which is dangerous to windows, cars and people and damages the auger. On gravel, leave a thin layer of snow, or use a tool with adjustable skid shoes instead of a power shovel.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Reaching Into a Clogged Chute</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Always stop the motor and remove the battery before clearing a clog. Use a stick or the supplied tool, never your hand, because packed wet snow can release suddenly when the auger frees. A silicone spray inside the chute before the season helps snow slide out cleanly.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

